course: dictionary
lesson: 11191
title: Showing longing with wistfully
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Wistfully» is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something, such as how they look, sigh, smile or speak. It usually shows a quiet longing, a wish for something you cannot have or something that is gone.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «The loyal Mole agrees — but adds, wistfully, that it sounds rather fun.» The word «but» and the way he adds it show that Mole would like to join in, even though he is not sure he can.
explain: Writers use «wistfully» to show a feeling without saying it outright. It often sits next to words like look, gaze, smile, sigh and say. When you meet it, try swapping in «longingly»: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Wistfully» means in a sad, longing way, as when you wish for something you cannot have or something that is gone.
fact: «Wistfully» can also mean in a quietly thoughtful, dreamy way, with a little sadness.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat gazes wistfully south after meeting the Sea Rat.
fact: A good check is to swap in a word like longingly and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The loyal Mole agrees — but adds, wistfully, that it sounds rather fun.» What does wistfully mean here? | Angrily and loudly | Quickly, in a hurry | *With a little longing, as if he wishes he could join in | Happily, without a care at all
exercise: Ellie looked wistfully at the toy in the shop window, but she had no money. Why does wistfully fit? | She is angry with the shop | She is hungry for lunch | *She wants the toy and cannot have it | She is dancing with joy
exercise: Priya says wistfully sounds like it means happily. What is her mistake? | She is right, it means happily | *It has a sad, longing feeling, not a happy one | It is a type of food | It means loudly
test: The old sailor smiled wistfully at the sea, remembering his home. How does he feel? | Very excited about a race | Cross with the sea | *Sad and longing for something gone | Bored and sleepy
test: Which sentence uses wistfully correctly? | *She gazed wistfully at the sea, wishing she could sail away. | He ate his lunch wistfully in a hurry. | He shouted wistfully at the referee. | The rain fell wistfully on the roof, loud and fast.
exam: Why do writers use wistfully instead of saying «he was sad and wanted something»? | To tell the reader the story has ended | *To show a feeling without saying it outright | To make the sentence longer for no reason | To mean the same as loudly
